


_list = [1, 4, 6, 234, 6, 764]# This line initializes a list called _list with six elements: 1, 4, 6, 234, 6, and 764. The list is a collection of items that can be of different data types, but in this case, all the elements are integers. We will use this list to demonstrate how to iterate over its elements using a for loop.  
for i in _list: # This line starts a for loop that will iterate over each element in the list _list. The variable i will take on the value of each element in the list, one at a time, as the loop runs. In the first iteration, i will be 1; in the second iteration, i will be 4; and so on, until it has iterated through all the elements in the list.
    print(i) # This line prints the current value of i to the console. Each time the loop runs, it will output the current element of the list that i is representing. As the loop iterates through the list, it will print each element in order: 1, 4, 6, 234, 6, and 764.    



#Same example using tuple
_tuple = (1, 4, 6, 234, 6, 764)
for i in _tuple: # This line starts a for loop that will iterate over each element in the tuple _tuple. The variable i will take on the value of each element in the tuple, one at a time, as the loop runs. In the first iteration, i will be 1; in the second iteration, i will be 4; and so on, until it has iterated through all the elements in the tuple.
    print(i) # This line prints the current value of i to the console. Each time the loop runs, it will output the current element of the tuple that i is representing. As the loop iterates through the tuple, it will print each element in order: 1, 4, 6, 234, 6, and 764.  



#same example using string
_string = "Hello"   
for i in _string: # This line starts a for loop that will iterate over each character in the string _string. The variable i will take on the value of each character in the string, one at a time, as the loop runs. In the first iteration, i will be 'H'; in the second iteration, i will be 'e'; and so on, until it has iterated through all the characters in the string.
    print(i) # This line prints the current value of i to the console. Each time the loop runs, it will output the current character of the string that i is representing. As the loop iterates through the string, it will print each character in order: 'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', and 'o'. 